__d(function(e,a,r,t,i,o,n){i.exports={version:"2.0",board:"Karnataka State Board / NCERT",grade:7,subject:"Mathematics",textbook:"Mathematics \u2014 Class 7 (NCERT)",chapter_number:11,chapter_title:"Perimeter and Area",total_cards:20,cards:[{id:"fc01",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"State the formula for the area of a parallelogram. What does 'height' mean here?",back:"Area of parallelogram = base (b) \xd7 height (h). The HEIGHT is the PERPENDICULAR distance between the two parallel sides (the base and its opposite side). It is NOT the slant side. The slant side is used only for the perimeter. Perimeter of parallelogram = 2(a + b), where a and b are adjacent sides.",memory_hook:"PARALLELOGRAM AREA: imagine pushing a rectangle sideways \u2014 it leans but the SAME area stays (base \xd7 perpendicular height). The slant is for perimeter only. BASE \xd7 PERPENDICULAR = AREA every time!"},{id:"fc02",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is the formula for the area of a triangle? How does it relate to the parallelogram?",back:"Area of triangle = \xbd \xd7 base \xd7 height. Relationship: a diagonal of a parallelogram divides it into two EQUAL triangles. Each triangle has exactly HALF the area of the parallelogram. So: Triangle area = \xbd \xd7 (Parallelogram area) = \xbd \xd7 base \xd7 height. The height is the perpendicular distance from the base to the opposite vertex.",memory_hook:"TRIANGLE = HALF a PARALLELOGRAM. Draw a diagonal in ANY parallelogram \u2014 two equal triangles appear! Each gets half. So triangle area = \xbd \xd7 b \xd7 h. Remember: \xbd is the magic fraction that makes triangles."},{id:"fc03",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"State the formula for circumference and area of a circle.",back:"Circumference (perimeter of circle) = 2\u03c0r = \u03c0d, where r = radius, d = diameter. Area of circle = \u03c0r\xb2. \u03c0 \u2248 22/7 (use when r is a multiple of 7) or \u03c0 \u2248 3.14 (otherwise). Example: radius = 7 cm \u2192 Circumference = 2 \xd7 22/7 \xd7 7 = 44 cm; Area = 22/7 \xd7 7\xb2 = 22/7 \xd7 49 = 154 cm\xb2.",memory_hook:"C = 2\u03c0r (go AROUND twice the radius, \u03c0 times). Area = \u03c0r\xb2 (SQUARE the radius, multiply by \u03c0). Trick: Circumference has the word 'ROUND' in it \u2014 2\u03c0r goes ROUND. Area gets SQUARED \u2014 \u03c0r\xb2!"},{id:"fc04",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is \u03c0 (pi)? What are its two commonly used approximations?",back:"\u03c0 (pi) is the mathematical constant equal to the ratio of any circle's circumference to its diameter: \u03c0 = C/d. It is an irrational number \u2014 it never ends and never repeats: \u03c0 = 3.14159265... Two approximations used in Class 7: (1) \u03c0 \u2248 22/7 \u2014 use when the radius is a multiple of 7 (e.g., 7, 14, 21, 35 cm); (2) \u03c0 \u2248 3.14 \u2014 use otherwise.",memory_hook:"\u03c0 = C/d = the constant ratio of EVERY circle's circumference to its diameter. Pi day is March 14 (3/14) because \u03c0 \u2248 3.14! Use 22/7 for multiples of 7 (clean division), use 3.14 for everything else."},{id:"fc05",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is the unit conversion between m\xb2 and cm\xb2? Between km\xb2 and m\xb2?",back:"1 m = 100 cm, so 1 m\xb2 = 100 \xd7 100 = 10,000 cm\xb2. To convert m\xb2 to cm\xb2: multiply by 10,000. To convert cm\xb2 to m\xb2: divide by 10,000. 1 km = 1000 m, so 1 km\xb2 = 1000 \xd7 1000 = 1,000,000 m\xb2 = 10\u2076 m\xb2. 1 hectare = 10,000 m\xb2 = 100 m \xd7 100 m. 1 km\xb2 = 100 hectares.",memory_hook:"UNIT SQUARE RULE: if 1 m = 100 cm, then 1 m\xb2 = 100\xb2 = 10,000 cm\xb2. ALWAYS SQUARE the conversion factor! Same for km: 1 km = 1000 m \u2192 1 km\xb2 = 1000\xb2 = 1,000,000 m\xb2. Square the number, square the result!"},{id:"fc06",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"How do you find the area of a path surrounding a rectangle?",back:"Steps: (1) Note the inner rectangle dimensions (l \xd7 b) and path width (w). (2) Outer length = l + 2w (path adds w on each end). (3) Outer width = b + 2w. (4) Outer area = (l+2w) \xd7 (b+2w). (5) Inner area = l \xd7 b. (6) Path area = Outer area \u2212 Inner area. KEY: the path width is added on BOTH sides (left+right for length; top+bottom for width), so total addition = 2w for each dimension.",memory_hook:"PATH adds to BOTH SIDES: +w on left +w on right = +2w total. Like adding a frame to a picture \u2014 the frame adds on ALL four sides. Outer = Inner + 2w (each dimension). Path area = Outer \u2212 Inner."},{id:"fc07",type:"Why/How",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Why is the height in a parallelogram's area formula the perpendicular height, not the slant side?",back:"Area represents the SPACE inside a shape. When you 'straighten up' a parallelogram into a rectangle (by cutting a right triangle from one end and moving it to the other), the base remains the same but the height becomes the VERTICAL (perpendicular) distance \u2014 NOT the slant side. The slant side is longer than the perpendicular height (it's the hypotenuse of the right triangle cut off). Using the slant side would overestimate the area.",memory_hook:"SLANT SIDE vs HEIGHT: slant side = hypotenuse (LONGER). Perpendicular height = the SHORT vertical distance. AREA uses the SHORT one (perpendicular). PERIMETER uses the LONG one (slant side). Never mix them up!"},{id:"fc08",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Find the circumference and area of a circle with radius = 21 cm. (Use \u03c0 = 22/7)",back:"Radius r = 21 cm. 21 is a multiple of 7, so use \u03c0 = 22/7. Circumference = 2\u03c0r = 2 \xd7 (22/7) \xd7 21 = 2 \xd7 22 \xd7 3 = 132 cm. Area = \u03c0r\xb2 = (22/7) \xd7 21 \xd7 21 = (22/7) \xd7 441 = 22 \xd7 63 = 1386 cm\xb2. Answer: Circumference = 132 cm, Area = 1386 cm\xb2.",memory_hook:"r = 21 = 3 \xd7 7 \u2192 use 22/7. 21 \xf7 7 = 3. Circumference: 2 \xd7 22 \xd7 3 = 132. Area: (22/7) \xd7 441 = 22 \xd7 63 = 1386. Whenever r is a multiple of 7, 22/7 simplifies beautifully!"},{id:"fc09",type:"Application",difficulty:"Moderate",front:"A parallelogram has base 18 cm and height 10 cm. A triangle has the same base and height. Compare their areas.",back:"Parallelogram area = base \xd7 height = 18 \xd7 10 = 180 cm\xb2. Triangle area = \xbd \xd7 base \xd7 height = \xbd \xd7 18 \xd7 10 = 90 cm\xb2. The triangle's area is exactly HALF the parallelogram's area when they share the same base and height. This confirms the relationship: triangle area = \xbd \xd7 parallelogram area (same base and height).",memory_hook:"SAME BASE + SAME HEIGHT: Triangle = \xbd \xd7 Parallelogram. Always. No exceptions. If parallelogram = 180, triangle = 90. This relationship is the CORE of why triangle area formula has \xbd!"},{id:"fc10",type:"Application",difficulty:"Moderate",front:"A square field has side 50 m. A 5 m wide path runs outside. Find the path area.",back:"Inner square: side = 50 m. Inner area = 50\xb2 = 2500 m\xb2. Path width = 5 m, so outer side = 50 + 5 + 5 = 60 m. Outer area = 60\xb2 = 3600 m\xb2. Path area = 3600 \u2212 2500 = 1100 m\xb2.",memory_hook:"Square with path: outer side = inner side + 2 \xd7 path width = 50 + 10 = 60. Path area = 60\xb2 \u2212 50\xb2 = 3600 \u2212 2500 = 1100 m\xb2. The difference of squares formula works here: 60\xb2 \u2212 50\xb2 = (60+50)(60\u221250) = 110 \xd7 10 = 1100!"},{id:"fc11",type:"Why/How",difficulty:"Moderate",front:"Two rectangles have the same area of 36 cm\xb2. Do they necessarily have the same perimeter? Give an example.",back:"No! Same area does NOT mean same perimeter. Example: Rectangle 1: 9 cm \xd7 4 cm. Area = 36 cm\xb2 \u2713. Perimeter = 2(9+4) = 26 cm. Rectangle 2: 12 cm \xd7 3 cm. Area = 36 cm\xb2 \u2713. Perimeter = 2(12+3) = 30 cm. Rectangle 3: 6 cm \xd7 6 cm (square). Area = 36 cm\xb2 \u2713. Perimeter = 24 cm. All three have the same area but different perimeters. The square has the MINIMUM perimeter for a given area (among rectangles).",memory_hook:"AREA = space inside. PERIMETER = boundary length. Same space can have DIFFERENT boundary lengths. A tall thin rectangle has the same area as a short fat one \u2014 but very different perimeters. Shape matters, not just size!"},{id:"fc12",type:"Application",difficulty:"Moderate",front:"A wheel has diameter 84 cm. How far does it travel in 100 complete rotations? (\u03c0 = 22/7)",back:"Diameter = 84 cm, so radius = 42 cm. Circumference = \u03c0d = (22/7) \xd7 84 = 22 \xd7 12 = 264 cm. In 1 rotation, the wheel travels 264 cm. In 100 rotations: 100 \xd7 264 = 26,400 cm = 264 m. Answer: The wheel travels 264 m in 100 rotations.",memory_hook:"WHEEL DISTANCE = circumference \xd7 number of rotations. Each rotation = one circumference length of road covered. 100 rotations = 100 \xd7 264 cm = 26,400 cm = 264 m. Bicycles, cars, trains \u2014 ALL use this principle!"},{id:"fc13",type:"Application",difficulty:"Moderate",front:"Find the area of a triangle with base 24 cm and height 15 cm.",back:"Area of triangle = \xbd \xd7 base \xd7 height = \xbd \xd7 24 \xd7 15 = \xbd \xd7 360 = 180 cm\xb2. Simple steps: multiply base and height first (24 \xd7 15 = 360), then divide by 2 (360 \xf7 2 = 180). Answer: 180 cm\xb2. Always double-check that the height given is perpendicular to the base.",memory_hook:"\xbd \xd7 24 \xd7 15 = 12 \xd7 15 = 180 cm\xb2. Shortcut: take HALF of the base first (24\xf72 = 12), then multiply by height (12 \xd7 15 = 180). One multiplication instead of two \u2014 always halve the easier number first!"},{id:"fc14",type:"Why/How",difficulty:"Moderate",front:"The circumference of a circle is 44 cm. Find its radius and area. (\u03c0 = 22/7)",back:"C = 2\u03c0r = 44. So 2 \xd7 (22/7) \xd7 r = 44. (44/7) \xd7 r = 44. r = 44 \xd7 7/44 = 7 cm. Area = \u03c0r\xb2 = (22/7) \xd7 7\xb2 = (22/7) \xd7 49 = 22 \xd7 7 = 154 cm\xb2. Answer: r = 7 cm, Area = 154 cm\xb2. Tip: always find radius FIRST from the given measurement, then calculate area.",memory_hook:"Circumference given \u2192 find r first. C = 2\u03c0r \u2192 r = C/(2\u03c0) = 44/(2 \xd7 22/7) = 44 \xd7 7/44 = 7. Then area = \u03c0 \xd7 7\xb2 = 154. Always work INWARD: circumference \u2192 radius \u2192 area!"},{id:"fc15",type:"Application",difficulty:"Hard",front:"A rectangular park 80 m \xd7 60 m has a 4 m wide path inside along its boundary. Find the area of the path.",back:"The path is INSIDE the rectangle. Inner dimensions (what's left after the path): length = 80 \u2212 4 \u2212 4 = 72 m; width = 60 \u2212 4 \u2212 4 = 52 m. Inner area = 72 \xd7 52 = 3744 m\xb2. Outer area = 80 \xd7 60 = 4800 m\xb2. Path area = 4800 \u2212 3744 = 1056 m\xb2.",memory_hook:"PATH INSIDE: subtract 2w from EACH dimension. 80 \u2212 2(4) = 72; 60 \u2212 2(4) = 52. Then: Outer \u2212 Inner = 4800 \u2212 3744 = 1056. PATH OUTSIDE adds 2w. PATH INSIDE subtracts 2w. Same formula, different direction!"},{id:"fc16",type:"Application",difficulty:"Hard",front:"A circular pond has radius 10 m. A 2 m wide path surrounds it. Find the area of the path. (\u03c0 = 3.14)",back:"Inner radius r = 10 m. Outer radius R = 10 + 2 = 12 m. Path area = \u03c0(R\xb2 \u2212 r\xb2) = \u03c0(12\xb2 \u2212 10\xb2) = \u03c0(144 \u2212 100) = \u03c0 \xd7 44 = 3.14 \xd7 44 = 138.16 m\xb2. Answer: Path area \u2248 138.16 m\xb2.",memory_hook:"CIRCULAR PATH area = \u03c0(R\xb2 \u2212 r\xb2) = \u03c0(outer\xb2 \u2212 inner\xb2). Outer radius = inner + path width. This is the RING formula \u2014 like the area of a ring/washer. Subtract the inner hole from the outer disk!"},{id:"fc17",type:"Application",difficulty:"Hard",front:"The area of a parallelogram is 150 cm\xb2 and its height is 10 cm. Find the base.",back:"Area = base \xd7 height. 150 = base \xd7 10. Base = 150 \xf7 10 = 15 cm. Answer: Base = 15 cm. This is a REVERSE problem \u2014 finding a dimension from the area. Always rearrange the formula: base = Area \xf7 height (when area and height are known).",memory_hook:"REVERSE FORMULA: if Area = b \xd7 h, then b = Area \xf7 h and h = Area \xf7 b. Triangle: if Area = \xbdbh, then b = 2\xd7Area\xf7h. Always rearrange the formula for the unknown. Formula triangles help: draw Area/b\xd7h and cover the unknown!"},{id:"fc18",type:"Application",difficulty:"Hard",front:"Convert: (a) 2.5 m\xb2 to cm\xb2 (b) 450,000 cm\xb2 to m\xb2.",back:"(a) 1 m\xb2 = 10,000 cm\xb2. So 2.5 m\xb2 = 2.5 \xd7 10,000 = 25,000 cm\xb2. (b) 1 m\xb2 = 10,000 cm\xb2, so 1 cm\xb2 = 1/10,000 m\xb2. Therefore 450,000 cm\xb2 = 450,000 \xf7 10,000 = 45 m\xb2. Answer: (a) 25,000 cm\xb2, (b) 45 m\xb2.",memory_hook:"m\xb2 TO cm\xb2: MULTIPLY by 10,000 (getting smaller unit = more of them). cm\xb2 TO m\xb2: DIVIDE by 10,000 (getting bigger unit = fewer of them). The conversion factor (10,000) comes from 100\xb2 \u2014 SQUARE the linear conversion!"},{id:"fc19",type:"Why/How",difficulty:"Hard",front:"Among all rectangles with the same perimeter, which has the maximum area?",back:"The SQUARE has the maximum area among all rectangles with the same perimeter. Example: Perimeter = 40 cm. Rectangle 1: 15\xd75, area = 75. Rectangle 2: 12\xd78, area = 96. Rectangle 3: 10\xd710 (square), area = 100. The square (all sides equal) gives the maximum area for a fixed perimeter. This is why most efficient containers are square/circular in cross-section \u2014 they enclose maximum area per unit perimeter.",memory_hook:"SQUARE = MOST AREA for given PERIMETER. The more SQUARE a rectangle, the more efficient. The MOST square rectangle IS a square! Nature uses this: circular/square containers waste the least material for the most volume."},{id:"fc20",type:"Why/How",difficulty:"Hard",front:"A garden is shaped like a right-angled triangle with legs 30 m and 40 m. A semicircular flowerbed of diameter 10 m is cut from the hypotenuse side. Find the remaining area.",back:"Step 1: Hypotenuse = \u221a(30\xb2 + 40\xb2) = \u221a(900+1600) = \u221a2500 = 50 m. Step 2: Triangle area = \xbd \xd7 30 \xd7 40 = 600 m\xb2. Step 3: Semicircle: diameter = 10 m \u2192 radius = 5 m. Area = \xbd \xd7 \u03c0 \xd7 r\xb2 = \xbd \xd7 3.14 \xd7 25 = 39.25 m\xb2. Step 4: Remaining area = 600 \u2212 39.25 = 560.75 m\xb2. This is a COMBINED SHAPES problem \u2014 subtract the removed shape from the main shape.",memory_hook:"COMBINED SHAPES: find each part's area separately, then add or subtract. Main shape \u2212 hole/removed shape = remaining. This appears in every real building plan \u2014 rooms subtracted from floors, windows from walls, flowerbeds from gardens!"}]}},2964,[]);